with d as (select value r from jsonb_array_elements($q$[{"notes":["Sorrel is best in spring and early summer. Grow it in a pot if you can, it comes back year after year.","The soup is best eaten the day it's made. Don't add the egg until just before serving, it doesn't keep well.","If you can't find sorrel, use spinach with a squeeze of lemon juice, though the flavour will be milder and less tart.","Food safety: Raw and lightly cooked foods aren't recommended for pregnant women, young children, older people or anyone with a weakened immune system."],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the oil in a large pot over medium heat. Add the diced onion and cook, stirring occasionally, until soft and pale golden, about 5 minutes."},{"text":"Add the potato and stock, and bring to a simmer. Cook, uncovered, for about 12 minutes, until the potato is tender."},{"text":"Add the sorrel and stir through. Cook for 2–3 minutes, just until the sorrel wilts and softens."},{"text":"In a small bowl, whisk the eggs together. Remove the pot from the heat and slowly pour the eggs into the hot (but no longer boiling) soup while stirring gently with a fork, this will create soft ribbons of cooked egg."},{"text":"Taste and season with salt and pepper. Stir through most of the fresh dill."},{"text":"Ladle into bowls and top with a spoonful of Greek yoghurt if you like, and a final sprinkle of fresh dill."}],"id":"660144d5-cc7e-457d-ae47-2983b629f455"},{"notes":["Ndolé has a pleasantly bitter taste, if you can't find it at an African grocer, spinach works but tastes gentler","Make this up to the point of adding the fish, cool quickly, and refrigerate for up to two days. Reheat gently and add fresh fish just before serving","Food safety: Cook fish until it is opaque and flakes easily (63°C in the thickest part), and prawns until pink and opaque all the way through."],"id":"00defa04-aac0-4962-ba77-530fd0edee1c"},{"notes":["You can prepare the onion, garlic and ginger up to 2 hours ahead. Store in small bowls, covered.","Frozen prawns work well; thaw them completely and pat dry before cooking so they don't release water into the curry.","Food safety: Cook prawns and other crustaceans until the shells turn pink and the flesh is opaque and firm."],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the mustard oil in a heavy-bottomed pan over medium-high heat. Once it's hot and fragrant, add the sliced onion and cook, stirring often, until golden and just beginning to crisp at the edges, about 6 minutes."},{"text":"Add the minced garlic and grated ginger to the pan and stir constantly for about 1 minute until fragrant. Be careful not to let it burn."},{"text":"Add the slit green chillies to the pan and cook for another minute, then add the ground turmeric and cumin. Stir well to coat the onions and release the spice aromas, about 30 seconds, until fragrant."},{"text":"Pour in the coconut cream and stir to combine. Bring the mixture to a gentle simmer and let it bubble softly for 3 to 4 minutes, allowing the flavours to meld."},{"text":"Slide the prawns into the simmering coconut curry and stir gently. Cook for 4 to 5 minutes, stirring occasionally, until the prawns turn pink and opaque throughout. Do not overcook or they will toughen."},{"text":"Taste and adjust the salt to your liking. If you prefer a thinner sauce, add a splash of warm water. Serve hot with steamed rice, garnished with fresh coriander if you like."}],"id":"2f1e3195-ff50-423b-a174-fa5598dc72e3"},{"notes":["Make it ahead: prepare in the morning, cover and refrigerate, then roast in the evening, just add 30 minutes to the cooking time.","Kai Māori: serve with boiled corn, pumpkin, or kūmara if you want it more like a proper boil-up.","Leftovers shred and freeze well for kai later in the week.","Food safety: Slow-cooked meat is safe once it is tender and the centre has reached at least 75°C."],"steps":[{"text":"Heat oven to 160°C. Pat the lamb shoulder dry and season well all over with salt and pepper."},{"text":"Heat oil in a large roasting pan over medium-high heat. Sear the lamb on all sides until golden brown, about 10 minutes total. Remove and set aside."},{"text":"In the same pan, toss the potatoes, carrots, pumpkin, onions and garlic together. Season with salt and pepper."},{"text":"Place the lamb back on top of the vegetables. Scatter rosemary over everything and pour water around the base."},{"text":"Cover the pan tightly with foil, then place a roasting pan lid or another baking tray on top to seal it well, this traps the steam like an earth oven would. Press the foil around the rim until the seal is tight and no steam escapes."},{"text":"Roast low and slow for 5 hours, until the lamb is fall-apart tender and the vegetables are soft and caramelised."},{"text":"Carefully remove the foil (watch the steam), then pull the meat from the bone. It is ready when it is tender and falls away easily. Serve everything together with the cooking liquid spooned over."}],"id":"0172ffa8-39b6-4fb9-aa05-1b679372ee09"},{"notes":["Samgyetang is traditionally eaten on Chobok, the hottest days of summer, to restore energy. Dong-hyun says it keeps for two days in the fridge, reheat gently before serving.","If you can find Korean red ginseng (홍삼), substitute 3 or 4 pieces for the fresh ginseng; they are denser and more assertive.","Use a chicken under 1 kg (a young bird); older birds take too long and become stringy.","Food safety: Cook chicken (and other poultry) until the thickest part reaches 75°C and the juices run clear, with no pink meat near the bone."],"steps":[{"text":"Bring the water to a boil in a large pot until it is at a steady rolling boil. Add the salt."},{"text":"Meanwhile, combine the glutinous rice, date halves, crushed garlic, and salt in a small bowl. Stuff the chicken cavity loosely with this mixture, do not pack it tight, as the rice will expand. Secure the opening with a cocktail pick if needed."},{"text":"Gently lower the whole chicken into the boiling salted water. Return to a boil, then reduce the heat to a bare simmer, with only a few small bubbles breaking the surface. Skim the surface once, at the beginning, until the scum is cleared."},{"text":"Add the ginseng, whole red dates, garlic cloves, ginger, and mushrooms to the broth around the chicken. Cover the pot and simmer gently for 1 hour and 45 minutes, until the chicken is very tender and cooked through (the thigh should reach 75°C inside). Do not stir, and add no more salt, the flavour is in the restraint."},{"text":"Add the spring onion whites in the last 5 minutes."},{"text":"Remove the chicken carefully with tongs and transfer to a serving bowl or to individual bowls. Ladle the hot broth over it. Serve the ginseng, dates, garlic, mushrooms, and ginger alongside in the bowl, for each person to enjoy."}],"id":"3ae6eb82-e8fb-474c-92d8-bdf5c418f730"},{"steps":[{"text":"Make the dipping sauce: whisk together soy sauce, rice vinegar, sesame oil, gochugaru if using, and garlic. Set aside."},{"text":"Make the batter: whisk flour, salt, water and egg together until smooth. Fold in the grated zucchini, carrot and spring onion, don't overmix."},{"text":"Heat 2 tbsp oil in a large frying pan over medium-high heat until it shimmers."},{"text":"Working in batches, pour the batter into the pan in rough 10 cm pancakes (they don't need to be perfect). Fry for 3 to 4 minutes until the edges are golden and crispy."},{"text":"Flip and fry for a further 2 minutes until golden on the second side and cooked through."},{"text":"Drain on paper towel and serve hot with dipping sauce."}],"id":"1d376942-f6c6-402b-a7c9-b23d179660a3"},{"notes":["Make the braise up to 2 days ahead and refrigerate; it actually tastes better the next day. Assemble the gratin and bake just before serving.","The reserved braising liquid is good in soups or risotto.","Any leftover gratin can be reheated gently in a 160°C oven, covered, until warm through.","Food safety: Slow-cooked meat is safe once it is tender and the centre has reached at least 75°C."],"steps":[{"text":"Heat the oil in a heavy-bottomed pot or casserole over medium-high heat. Season the oxtail pieces with salt and pepper, then brown them in batches, about 3 minutes per side. Don't crowd the pot. Set the meat aside."},{"text":"In the same pot, sauté the onions, carrots and celery until lightly golden, about 8 minutes. Add the garlic, stir, then add the tomato paste and cook for 2 minutes, stirring constantly."},{"text":"Pour in the red wine, scraping up any brown bits from the bottom of the pot. Add the beef stock, bay leaves and thyme. Return the oxtail to the pot and bring to a simmer. Cover and braise in a 160°C oven for 3 hours, until the meat is very tender and falls easily from the bone."},{"text":"Remove the pot from the oven and let cool slightly. Strain the braising liquid through a fine sieve, pressing gently on the vegetables to extract all the liquid. Pick the meat from the bones, discarding the bone and any fat. You should have about 400–500 g of shredded meat and about 600 ml of braising liquid."},{"text":"Make the béchamel: melt the butter in a saucepan over medium heat. Whisk in the flour and cook for 2 minutes, stirring constantly, don't let it brown. Gradually whisk in the warm milk, whisking constantly to avoid lumps. Cook, stirring often, until the sauce is thick and smooth, about 8 minutes. Season with salt, white pepper and nutmeg."},{"text":"Combine the shredded oxtail meat with 300 ml of the braising liquid (reserve the rest for another use). Gently fold in the béchamel sauce until just combined."},{"text":"Transfer the mixture to a gratin dish. Scatter the Gruyère evenly over the top. Bake at 200°C until the gratin is bubbling around the edges and the cheese is golden brown, about 30–40 minutes. Let rest for 5 minutes before serving."}],"id":"4d39f4e8-95e1-4c91-a089-66d38bb77917"},{"notes":["Bitso-bitso means 'this and that' in Kapampangan, here, a mix of offal. You can add pork kidney (about 150 g, trimmed and halved) if you like.","The adobo keeps well in the fridge for up to 3 days. The flavour deepens overnight.","Fresh offal is essential. Ask your butcher for the freshest pork liver and heart available."],"id":"f239a922-be5d-4158-997e-f7e4b073b358"},{"notes":["Make it a day ahead: cool, cover, and refrigerate. Reheat gently on the stove, adding a splash of water if it's too thick.","Use bone-in lamb shoulder if you can find it, the bones add flavour.","Food safety: Braised beef and lamb cook well past 75°C on the way to tender, so they are safe once soft."],"id":"e167b121-3807-458b-9814-24c5bcac2971"},{"notes":["You can prep the parcels a few hours ahead, cover them with a damp cloth, and steam them just before serving.","If you can't find banana leaves, use parchment paper, it won't look as pretty but tastes the same."],"id":"308561f3-7397-4eec-8107-9c0b8b3b08bc"},{"notes":["Make sure your rice is cooled first, this stops it from becoming mushy. Leftover rice from yesterday works perfectly.","If you don't have tinned tomatoes, use 150 g of fresh tomato, chopped, or 2 tbsp tomato paste mixed with 2 tbsp water.","Food safety: Raw and lightly cooked foods aren't recommended for pregnant women, young children, older people or anyone with a weakened immune system. Cool any leftover rice quickly and refrigerate it within an hour; reheat it until steaming hot."],"id":"1006703c-41be-4fae-960f-0d9d3871165f"},{"notes":["You can cook the pork up to a day ahead and refrigerate it, this actually makes slicing cleaner. Reheat gently in the wok before adding the bean paste.","The doubanjiang (or doubanjiang with chilli if you prefer a little more warmth) is your backbone here; don't skip it or use substitutes. It's what makes this dish sing.","Serve with plain steamed rice to balance the rich, umami sauce.","Food safety: Cook fresh pork to at least 70°C in the thickest part."],"steps":[{"text":"Cut the pork belly into thin slices, about 3 mm thick (easier if the pork is slightly cold). Simmer the pork in a pot of water for 20 minutes until just cooked through, then drain and set aside on a plate."},{"text":"Heat 1 tbsp neutral oil in a wok or large frying pan over medium heat. Add the spring onion whites, garlic and ginger, stirring for about 1 minute until fragrant."},{"text":"Add the doubanjiang and stir constantly for 2 minutes, breaking it up as it heats, until the paste darkens slightly and releases its aroma."},{"text":"Add the cooked pork slices and toss gently but thoroughly, coating each piece in the bean paste, for about 3 minutes."},{"text":"Add the capsicums, soy sauce, sugar and a pinch of salt. Cook, stirring often, for 8 to 10 minutes until the peppers are tender and the sauce is glossy and clingy."},{"text":"Add the spring onion greens, drizzle with sesame oil, toss once and taste, adjust salt if needed. Serve hot with steamed rice."}],"id":"d2686fc2-58e3-45ba-947f-a84f5f1679dc"},{"notes":["Make sure you don't lift the lid while the rice is cooking, opening it lets the steam out and stops the rice cooking evenly.","This keeps in the fridge for up to three days. Reheat gently in a rice cooker or microwave with a splash of water."],"id":"a2e522d8-cc1f-4161-8545-3fd1a29b2eec"},{"notes":["You can stir in sautéed mushrooms or crispy bacon to the eggs before baking if you like.","The æggeost is best served warm, but it's also nice cold for lunch the next day.","Food safety: Raw and lightly cooked foods aren't recommended for pregnant women, young children, older people or anyone with a weakened immune system."],"id":"a3f9afea-38be-44e4-b49f-21eb0f0610f7"},{"notes":["Kibbeh can be shaped up to 4 hours ahead and refrigerated, but don't freeze before frying, the texture gets grainy.","If the mixture is too sticky to work with, add a little more bulgur; if it's too dry, add cold water a teaspoon at a time.","The filling must be completely cool before you stuff, or it will start cooking the shell from inside.","Food safety: Cook mince and sausages until no pink remains and the centre reaches 75°C."],"steps":[{"text":"Make the filling first: heat the olive oil in a large pan over medium-high heat. Add the chopped onion and cook until soft and golden, about 4 minutes. Add the mince, breaking it up as it cooks, and stir for 5–6 minutes until cooked through. Add the pine nuts, cinnamon and cloves, season with salt and pepper, and stir for another minute. Set aside to cool completely."},{"text":"Meanwhile, rinse the bulgur under cold water until the water runs clear, then drain well. In a bowl, combine the bulgur, 500 g mince, chopped onion, allspice and cinnamon. Season with salt and pepper. Add cold water a little at a time, mixing with your hands, until the mixture holds together like a dough, you want it moist but not wet. Cover and rest for 10 minutes."},{"text":"Wet your hands with cold water. Take a handful of the bulgur mixture (about the size of a golf ball) and shape it into an oval. Push your thumb into the centre to make a cavity, then gently hollow it out, rotating it in your palm, until the walls are thin. Fill with about 1 tbsp of the cooled filling, then pinch and seal the opening, gently smoothing the seam. Shape into an oval or pointed torpedo. Repeat with the remaining mixture."},{"text":"Heat the oil to 160°C in a deep heavy-based pot or wok (use a thermometer to check). Fry the kibbeh in batches, without crowding the pan, for 4–5 minutes, turning halfway, until golden brown and crispy all over. Do not let the oil get too hot or they'll brown before cooking through. Remove with a slotted spoon and drain on paper towel."},{"text":"Serve the kibbeh warm with Greek yoghurt and lemon wedges on the side."}],"id":"b6db635e-dffa-49e3-8ae9-81ce14595183"}]$q$::jsonb)), up as (update public.published_recipes p set recipe = p.recipe || (d.r - 'id' - 'notes') || case when d.r ? 'notes' then jsonb_build_object('notes', coalesce((select jsonb_agg(n) from jsonb_array_elements(d.r->'notes') n where btrim(n#>>'{}') <> 'Food safety:'),'[]'::jsonb)) else '{}'::jsonb end, diets = case when d.r ? 'diets' then array(select jsonb_array_elements_text(d.r->'diets')) else p.diets end from d where p.id=(d.r->>'id')::uuid returning p.id) select (select count(*) from d) expected, (select count(*) from up) updated;